"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, and lack of lymphovascular invasion. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 13.5. Conversely, the surgical pathology showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological stage pT2c. The absence of documented nodal sampling introduces uncertainty, as pNx does not equate to pN0. Biopsy findings were consistent with the prostatectomy specimen. The difference between biopsy and prostatectomy findings is not explicitly detailed beyond the overall pathological staging. Missing postoperative PSA measurements and follow-up data significantly widen the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their trend is crucial for refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.55 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 41.73–97.37 months; its coverage has not been validated."
